# kaiten-reference

Users can keep a local file with the Kaiten entity IDs, metadata, and working preferences they
work with (spaces, boards, columns, lanes, card types, custom properties, users). Its purpose is
to make agents work with the entities that already exist, exactly as the user set them up, instead
of mis-resolving or duplicating them. Default location:
`~/.config/kaiten/reference.<ext>` next to the config, any extension. An optional `reference` key
in `~/.config/kaiten/config.json` points anywhere else. The CLI ignores both — they are for agents.

## Why the file outranks the API

Kaiten instances accumulate same-named spaces, boards, and card types. Resolving by name through
the API can silently return the wrong duplicate. IDs a human verified and wrote down do not have
this problem — when the file has the ID, use it and do not re-resolve it.

Notes, rules, and defaults written in the file are the user's configuration — follow them, and
work with the entities it names instead of creating parallel ones.

## No file yet? Offer to start one

The moment a task forces an API lookup and no reference file exists, tell the user what you had to
look up and offer to start the file with it — default path `~/.config/kaiten/reference.md` unless
the user prefers another path (then also set the `reference` key) or format. Seed only with IDs
the user confirms; never bulk-dump the API into it.

## The file belongs to the user

Any format goes — JSON, YAML, Markdown, plain text. Match the existing format when appending.
Never reformat, restructure, or delete entries unless the user asks for exactly that.

## Keep it growing — with approval

1. Read the file before writing — check the data is not already there.
2. If the API taught you an ID the file lacks, propose the exact addition — show the lines you
   intend to write.
3. Append only after explicit user approval. An unverified ID poisons every future session that
   trusts the file.
